<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=nuclear-fission">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuclear fission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nuclear fission is a reaction in which the nucleus of an atom splits into two or more smaller nuclei, releasing a very large amount of energy; it was discovered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in December 1938, when the chemists Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann found barium among the products of uranium bombarded with neutrons. Lise Meitner and Otto Robert Frisch explained the result in January 1939, and Frisch named the process after the division of living cells. The extra neutrons it releases make a chain reaction possible, the basis of nuclear power and nuclear weapons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuclear-fission.jpg" alt="Nuclear fission" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J Brew · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuclear_Fission_Experimental_Apparatus_1938_-_Deutsches_Museum_-_Munich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1938</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Berlin</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Otto Hahn · Fritz Strassmann · Lise Meitner · Otto Robert Frisch</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Hahn and Strassmann found the evidence in Berlin; Meitner and Frisch, who explained it in January 1939, were then in exile.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/radioactivity">Radioactivity<span>1896</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">Quantum theory<span>1900</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/chicago-pile-1">Nuclear reactor<span>1942</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Atomic bomb<span>1945</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=nuclear-fission">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
